    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on my sociology thesis and I'm getting a bit bogged down in the results section. Could you explain the practical difference between a p-value and a confidence interval? Specifically, if I have a p-value of 0.04 and a 95% confidence interval that doesn't include zero for my coefficient, how should I describe the significance of these findings in a formal paper?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The p-value measures the probability of observing your data assuming the null hypothesis is true, while a confidence interval provides a plausible range for the true population effect size. In your case, both results indicate statistical significance at the p < 0.05 level because the interval excludes zero and the p-value is below the standard threshold. You should describe this by stating that your effect is statistically significant and present the coefficient alongside its interval to convey both the precision of the estimate and the magnitude of the effect.",
      "source": "target",
